From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard Spindler <richard@lateralblast.com.au>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 22/55] compression: add huff0@hpnssh.org, a dictionary-free
 method

OpenSSH now restricts zlib compression to Huffman coding only, so the
dictionary shared by all channels of a session cannot leak one channel's data
into another's packet lengths. That closed the attack but left Compression yes
slow: zlib still runs all of deflate for a coder that is only a byte count and
a table lookup, 59 MB/s on a LAN and 51 MB/s on a 1 Gbit/s path with 50 ms
delay, below the 94 MB/s of no compression at all.

Add a compression method, huff0@hpnssh.org, that does the same coding with the
Huffman coder from zstd (huff0). Each payload is coded on its own, in blocks of
up to 128 KiB with a new table for each, and nothing is kept between packets,
so there is still no dictionary. A block that does not shrink is stored, and a
run of one byte is a single byte. The format is described at the top of
compress-huff0.c.

It is offered after the other two. The client puts it first when Compression is
yes, as zlib@openssh.com was, and a server that does not know the name picks
zlib as before: an older HPN-SSH client or server, or OpenSSH, falls back to
zlib@openssh.com without a change on its side. Like zlib it starts only after
authentication (COMP_DELAYED), so the decoder is not reachable before login.
It does not need zlib at build time.

The coder is copied from zstd 1.5.7 into huff0/ (BSD or GPLv2, both copied);
README.md there lists the small changes needed to build it on its own.
compress-huff0.c is the wrapper. The decoder checks every block's lengths
against the packet size limit before it writes, and handles the one-byte run
case itself because HUF_decompress4X_hufOnly_wksp does not.

Measured on an x86-64 server over emulated links, aes256-gcm, HPN-SSH on both
ends, 3 runs, MB/s of source data (log-like text that gzip shrinks 4.2 to 1,
and random data):

  link               data          none    zlib   huff0
  LAN                text           580     59     253
  LAN                random         625     68     417
  1 Gbit/s, 50 ms    text            94     51     131
  1 Gbit/s, 50 ms    random          94     59      94
  100 Mbit/s, 50 ms  text          11.3   17.0    17.0
  10 Mbit/s, 50 ms   text           1.2    1.8     1.8

It sends the same 65 percent of the text as zlib does, so on a link the
compression helps with it is as fast as zlib, and on a fast link it no longer
gets in the way of an incompressible transfer: client CPU on the LAN fell from
17.3 to 4.2 seconds per GB of text. An uncompressed LAN transfer is still more
than twice as fast, so Compression yes remains the wrong choice there.

tests/c/huff0_roundtrip.c checks round trips at sizes from 0 to 256 KiB for
four kinds of data, the output sizes, malformed streams and 4000 corrupted
ones, and is clean under AddressSanitizer and UBSan. A loopback run against a
build without the method (zlib is chosen) and one against OpenSSH (likewise)
confirmed the fallback.
